---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { itemList } from '../../lib/schema';

const PATH = '/compare/';
const PAGES = [
  { name: 'Personality tests without signup', path: '/compare/personality-tests-without-signup/', blurb: 'What the email gate costs you, and what a genuinely ungated test looks like.' },
  { name: 'Free vs paid personality tests', path: '/compare/free-vs-paid-personality-tests/', blurb: 'What you actually get for the money, and the cases where paying is the right call.' },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Comparing Personality Tests: What to Look For"
  description="How free personality tests differ in what they gate, what they charge for and what they measure — plus the questions worth asking before you start answering."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Compare', path: PATH }]}
  schema={[itemList('Personality test comparisons', PAGES.map((p) => ({ name: p.name, path: p.path })))]}
>
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">Comparisons</p>
    <h1>Comparing personality tests</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Free personality tests differ less in their questions than in their business model.
      Understanding which model you are dealing with tells you most of what you need to know
      before you start answering.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="grid grid-2">
      {PAGES.map((page) => (
        <article class="card">
          <h2 style="font-size:1.2rem;margin-top:0"><a href={page.path}>{page.name}</a></h2>
          <p>{page.blurb}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section tint">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>The four business models behind "free" tests</h2>
      <p>Nearly every free personality test on the web runs on one of these.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <caption>What the site gets in exchange for the test</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Model</th><th scope="col">What you give up</th><th scope="col">How to spot it</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Email gate</th>
              <td>Your address, and the marketing that follows it.</td>
              <td>A signup step appears after the questions, never before.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Freemium report</th>
              <td>The useful half of your result, unless you pay.</td>
              <td>A summary result with sections marked "premium" or blurred.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Advertising</th>
              <td>Attention, page speed, and some tracking.</td>
              <td>Display ads, or a result split across several pages.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Genuinely free</th>
              <td>Nothing, which is rarer than the word "free" suggests.</td>
              <td>No signup, no paid tier, no ads, results on screen immediately.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Xenapsis is the fourth. There is no email step, no paid tier, and no advertising —
        the site is static files and the scoring runs in your browser, so there is very little
        for it to cost.
      </p>

      <h2>Five questions worth asking before you start</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Does the result appear without an email?</strong> Check the FAQ before spending ten minutes answering questions.</li>
        <li><strong>Is the full result free, or a teaser?</strong> A "free test" advertised next to a report price usually means the free part is a summary.</li>
        <li><strong>Does it report strength, or just a label?</strong> A bare four-letter code hides whether your result was decisive or a coin toss. <a href="/guides/what-do-personality-test-percentages-mean/">Why that matters.</a></li>
        <li><strong>Are the questions balanced?</strong> If every statement is worded in the same direction, agreeing with all of them produces an extreme result that means nothing. <a href="/methodology/">How we handle this.</a></li>
        <li><strong>Is the site honest about limits?</strong> Any test claiming to predict job performance from type is overselling. <a href="/guides/are-personality-tests-accurate/">What the research supports.</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
